forge dev                     # rebuilds and restarts when files change; or: cargo run
```

`serve` requires `FORGE_DATABASE_URL`; a URL for another database engine exits
with code 78.
%%if !sqlite%%

With `FORGE_ENV=production` (the container image's default) both URLs must
verify the server's TLS certificate, or start-up stops and says how to fix
the URL:

```bash
%%if postgresql%%
export FORGE_DATABASE_URL='postgres://app_runtime:PASSWORD@db.internal:5432/DATABASE?sslmode=verify-full'
# private CA: append &sslrootcert=/etc/ssl/db-ca.pem
%%end%%
%%if !postgresql%%
export FORGE_DATABASE_URL='mysql://app:PASSWORD@db.internal:3306/DATABASE?ssl-mode=VERIFY_IDENTITY'
# private CA: append &ssl-ca=/etc/ssl/db-ca.pem
%%end%%
# only for a database reached over a trusted private network:
export FORGE_DATABASE_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=true
```
%%if postgresql%%

Production also refuses a database role that could bypass row-level
security: `serve`, `jobs work` and the `jobs` commands exit 1, naming the
role, when the role `FORGE_DATABASE_URL` logs in as, or acts as, is a
superuser, has `BYPASSRLS`, `CREATEROLE` or `REPLICATION`, is a member of
such a role or of `pg_execute_server_program`, `pg_write_server_files` or
`pg_read_server_files`, owns (or can `SET ROLE` to the owner of) a table with
row-level security, or when the URL switches it to another role
(`options=-c role=...`). Serve as the runtime role, never granted the
migration role, and migrate as the migration role:

```bash
docker compose up -d db
FORGE_ENV=production FORGE_DATABASE_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=true \
  FORGE_DATABASE_URL='postgres://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/%%DATABASE_NAME%%' \
  cargo run -- jobs status   # refused: PostgreSQL role "postgres", which is a superuser
FORGE_ENV=production FORGE_DATABASE_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=true \
  FORGE_DATABASE_URL='%%DATABASE_RUNTIME_URL_LOCAL%%' \
  cargo run -- jobs status   # accepted: the runtime role
```

The repository tests `forge generate scaffold` writes run as a fresh runtime
role with the same privileges and prove it cannot change the schema:

```bash
FORGE_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/postgres forge test -- --include-ignored
```
%%end%%

The serving pool is tuned with `FORGE_DATABASE_MAX_CONNS` (default `10`),
`FORGE_DATABASE_CONNECT_TIMEOUT` (default `5s`) and
`FORGE_DATABASE_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT` (default `30s`, cancels longer statements;
migrations and commands run without it).
%%end%%

The `forge db` commands run this application's own subcommands (the CLI builds
it with Cargo and never reads the database URL), like Rails' `db:*` tasks:

```bash
forge db status               # applied and pending migrations; or: cargo run -- migrate status
forge db rollback --steps 1   # your newest migration, never a Forge-owned table; or: cargo run -- rollback --steps 1
forge db setup                # create + migrate + seed
forge db reset                # drop + setup: a clean development database
forge db drop                 # or: cargo run -- db drop
forge doctor                  # toolchain, FORGE_* settings, is the database server answering?
```

`drop`, `reset` and `db rollback` are refused when `FORGE_ENV` is not
`development` or `test` (and `drop` also when the server is not on this
machine) unless you pass `--force-production` and either type the application
name at the prompt or pass `--yes` with `FORGE_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE=true` in the
process environment. `db create` and `db drop` use
`FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL`; its role needs the privilege to create or drop
databases (the Compose migration role has it).

%%if postgresql%%
Database-enabled applications include `infrastructure::audit::PgAuditSink`,
`infrastructure::auth::PostgresSessionStore`, and
`infrastructure::tenancy::PgMembershipStore`. The runtime role can append
structured security-audit events but cannot read, update, or delete audit
history.

%%end%%
Create a reversible application migration with:

```bash
forge generate migration create_customers
```

This creates matching `.up.sql` and `.down.sql` files in `migrations/`.
Migration SQL is checked out with LF line endings so SQLx hashes remain stable
across operating systems.
%%if views%%

Generate an HTML CRUD resource (migration, entity, use cases, repository,
handlers, views and tests) with:

```bash
forge generate scaffold post title:string body:text published:boolean --global
```

The generator only creates files; it prints the `pub mod` lines and the
`compose` statements (in `src/bootstrap/mod.rs`) that wire the resource. Its
routes answer `403` until you choose who may use them in
`src/adapters/http/access.rs`.

Add `--broadcast` for live updates: open index pages apply every create,
update and delete over Server-Sent Events (Turbo Streams). Production needs
the stream signing key, the same on every replica:

```bash
forge generate scaffold event name:string starts_at:timestamp --tenant-scoped --broadcast
export FORGE_STREAMS_KEY=$(openssl rand -base64 32)
```

That is why `Cargo.toml` enables the `broadcast` feature of `forge`: it only
compiles the live-update code, and nothing is served, connected or started
until you add the printed `Streams::register` wiring. If this application
will never broadcast, you may remove `"broadcast"` from the features.
%%end%%
%%if authentication%%

### Sign in

The application has sign-in, sign-out, password-reset and two-factor pages
(`src/adapters/http/authentication.rs`, `templates/sessions/`,
`templates/passwords/`, `templates/mfa/`), like `rails generate authentication`. Create the
first tenant and its owner with the migration role, then sign in:

```bash
export FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL='%%DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL_LOCAL%%'
forge setup --tenant acme --email owner@example.com --organization "Acme Inc"
# or: cargo run -- setup --tenant acme --email owner@example.com --organization "Acme Inc"
unset FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL
export FORGE_DATABASE_URL='%%DATABASE_RUNTIME_URL_LOCAL%%'
forge dev                     # or: cargo run
# open http://127.0.0.1:3000/session/new?tenant=acme
```

`setup` asks for the password twice with echo off (or reads one line with
`--password-stdin`, or `FORGE_SETUP_PASSWORD`; never an argument), needs at
least 12 characters, and refuses once a tenant exists. The owner gets the
`owner` role; tenant-scoped scaffolds grant it their permissions and send
signed-out browsers to the sign-in page.

### Password-reset emails

Reset links are emailed once outgoing mail is configured; until then the
reset page answers the same and delivers nothing. In development, write the
emails to `.eml` files:

```bash
export FORGE_MAIL_URL=file:tmp/mail
export FORGE_MAIL_FROM="%%APP_NAME%% <no-reply@example.com>"
export FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL=http://localhost:3000/passwords/edit
cargo run
# request a reset at http://127.0.0.1:3000/passwords/new?tenant=acme,
# then open the link in the newest tmp/mail/*.eml
```

Preview the email in the browser without sending anything (development and
test only):

```bash
FORGE_MAIL_PREVIEW=true cargo run
# open http://127.0.0.1:3000/_mail
```

In production use your SMTP server: STARTTLS on 587 with `smtp://`, implicit
TLS on 465 with `smtps://` (percent-encode `@`, `:` and `/` in the password).
Certificates are always verified; a private relay's CA goes in
`FORGE_MAIL_TLS_CA_FILE`.

```bash
export FORGE_MAIL_URL='smtps://user:password@smtp.example.com:465'
export FORGE_MAIL_FROM="%%APP_NAME%% <no-reply@example.com>"
export FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL=https://app.example.com/passwords/edit
```

`src/bootstrap/mod.rs` wires `forge::mail::password_reset_notifier` from
these variables; `FORGE_MAIL_URL` without `FORGE_AUTH_PASSWORD_RESET_URL`
stops the application at startup. Send your own email through the
`forge::mail::Sender` port that `forge::mail::from_config` builds, and test
it with `forge::mail::testing::Recorder` (see the Forge mail guide,
`docs/mail.md`).

### Two-factor authentication

Accounts can require a code from an authenticator app (TOTP, RFC 6238) at
every sign-in. Seeds are encrypted with `FORGE_AUTH_MFA_KEY`, canonical
unpadded base64url of 32 random bytes; keep it in your secret manager, never
in the database or the repository:

```bash
export FORGE_AUTH_MFA_KEY=$(openssl rand -base64 32 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '=')
forge dev                     # or: cargo run
# sign in, open http://127.0.0.1:3000/mfa, enter your password,
# add the key to your authenticator app and confirm with its code
```

| Method | Path | Page |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `GET`, `POST` | `/session/mfa` | The code step of a sign-in (authenticator or backup code) |
| `GET` | `/mfa` | Two-factor settings of the signed-in account |
| `POST` | `/mfa`, `/mfa/confirm` | Turn on (password), then confirm with the password and a code; shows 10 backup codes once |
| `POST` | `/mfa/rotation`, `/mfa/rotation/confirm` | Replace the authenticator (password and current code, again when confirming) |
| `POST` | `/mfa/backup_codes` | New backup codes (password and current code) |
| `DELETE` | `/mfa` | Turn off (password and current code) |

A code, or a backup code, works once; the code step is limited to five
attempts per account and 60 per network address in five minutes. Turning
two-factor on, replacing the authenticator and turning it off sign the
account out everywhere. An invalid key stops startup; without a key the
settings page says two-factor is unavailable and an account that has it
cannot sign in. With Docker, export the key before `docker compose up`.
%%end%%

