```bash
forge dev                     # serves on http://127.0.0.1:3000; or: cargo run
curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/health/live
forge check && forge lint && forge test
forge doctor                  # toolchain, FORGE_* settings and database reachability
```

`forge dev` rebuilds and restarts the application when sources, templates,
assets or migrations change: the old process keeps serving until the new build
succeeds, a failed build is printed and leaves it running, and Ctrl-C stops it
gracefully. `forge dev --no-reload` builds and runs once; `forge test ARGS`
passes `ARGS` to `cargo test`; `forge build` is the locked release build.

Binary commands: `serve` (default), `healthcheck`, `openapi`, `routes`, `version`, `help`.
%%if postgresql%%
`jobs work|status|dead|retry|discard` run and inspect the job queue (see
Background jobs).
%%end%%
%%if database%%
Database-enabled binaries also support `migrate [status]`, `rollback [--steps N]`
and `db create|exists|drop` (what `forge migrate`, `forge rollback` and
`forge db ...` run).
`migrate` applies pending embedded migrations and `migrate status` lists them;
`rollback` reverts the application's latest reversible migration (`--steps N`
reverts N), never one shipped by Forge (such as
the cache or rate-limit tables). `seed` runs `seed` in `src/bootstrap/seeds.rs` in one
transaction with `FORGE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL`; keep it idempotent.
`db drop` refuses `FORGE_ENV=production` and servers that are not on this
machine unless `--force-production` is given; `db exists` exits 3 when the
database is missing.
%%end%%
%%if authentication%%
`setup` creates the first tenant and its owner (see Sign in); `forge setup`
runs it.
%%end%%

Configuration uses `FORGE_*` environment variables; unknown `FORGE_*` keys are
rejected at startup. Run `cargo run -- help` for the full list.

Behind a reverse proxy (nginx, Traefik, a load balancer), list its networks in
`FORGE_HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXIES` so throttles, rate limits and live-update caps
count each client (`Request::client_ip`) instead of the proxy. Empty, the
default, trusts nobody; `/0`, IPv4 prefixes shorter than `/8`, IPv6 prefixes
shorter than `/16` and malformed entries stop startup. Only `X-Forwarded-For`
from a listed peer is read, from the right; keep the application port
reachable only through the proxy:

```bash
export FORGE_HTTP_TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.30.0.0/24,fd00:1::/64   # proxy CIDRs or addresses
cargo run
```
%%if postgresql%%

## Background jobs

`src/bootstrap/mod.rs` declares the persistent job queue (`forge::jobs`,
[jobs guide](https://github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-rust/blob/main/docs/jobs.md)),
like Solid Queue in Rails 8: `migrate` creates its tables, and `serve` runs
its workers, cron schedules and the pruning of finished jobs next to the HTTP
server, with the same lifecycle. On `SIGTERM` or Ctrl-C they stop claiming,
wait up to 20 seconds for running jobs, cancel the rest and hand them back to
the queue. Each queue runs at most 4 jobs at once (`jobs_configure`). No job
is registered yet; add one with:

```bash
forge generate job send_welcome user_id:uuid --queue --dry-run # preview the job, its handler and tests
forge generate job send_welcome user_id:uuid --queue  # src/adapters/jobs/send_welcome.rs and tests/send_welcome_job.rs
# add the printed `pub mod` lines and the `register` line at the end of `jobs_configure`,
# write `perform`, then:
forge test
```

Enqueue a job in the transaction that writes the business state it follows
from, so both commit or neither does:
`crate::adapters::jobs::send_welcome::enqueue(&mut transaction, &job, forge::jobs::EnqueueOptions::new())`.
Delivery is at least once: make `perform` idempotent. Failed attempts are
retried with backoff; a job out of attempts is `dead` until you act:

```bash
cargo run -- jobs status                # jobs per queue and state
cargo run -- jobs dead 20               # dead jobs, newest first (payloads are never shown)
cargo run -- jobs retry <JOB_ID>        # run a dead job again with a fresh attempt budget
cargo run -- jobs discard <JOB_ID>      # delete a dead job
forge destroy job send_welcome user_id:uuid --queue  # remove the generated files again
```

To scale web and worker processes separately, change
`.jobs(forge::WorkerMode::InServe)` to `forge::WorkerMode::Dedicated` in
`src/bootstrap/mod.rs`; `serve` then only enqueues and the workers run in:

```bash
cargo run -- jobs work                  # workers and cron schedules, without HTTP
```

The job migration grants the queue to the database role that serves requests,
`app_runtime` (what Compose creates). If your production role has another
name, set `forge.runtime_role` to it for the migration (the role must exist, or
the migration fails). Without it, and with no role called `app_runtime`, the
migration grants nothing and every worker logs `claiming jobs failed` about
once a second. A migration role that is not a superuser cannot persist the
setting with `ALTER DATABASE`, but it can pass it for one run:

```bash
PGOPTIONS='-c forge.runtime_role=myapp_runtime' forge migrate
```

A superuser may instead run `ALTER DATABASE %%DATABASE_NAME%% SET
forge.runtime_role = 'myapp_runtime'` once. `forge.worker_role` (default: the
runtime role) names a separate role for `jobs work` and the operator commands
above; see the
[jobs guide](https://github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-rust/blob/main/docs/jobs.md#database-roles-and-rls).
%%end%%

## OpenAPI

JSON routes describe themselves in an OpenAPI 3.1 document built from their
Rust types: `GET /v1/status` (`src/adapters/http/status.rs`) uses
`forge::openapi::typed`, and the health routes are described by Forge.
`tests/openapi.rs` checks every JSON response against the document.

```bash
curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/v1/status
curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/openapi.json        # development and test only, by default
forge openapi                                   # print the document (or: cargo run -- openapi)
forge openapi --output openapi.json             # write it, to commit and diff
forge openapi --check openapi.json              # exit 1 when it is out of date (CI)
FORGE_ENV=production FORGE_OPENAPI_ENABLED=true cargo run   # serve it in production too
```

The document lists every described route with its permission, so production
does not serve it unless `FORGE_OPENAPI_ENABLED=true`; `false` never serves
it. Describe your own JSON routes with `#[derive(OpenApiSchema)]` next to
`Serialize` and `forge::openapi::typed`, or attach an `Operation` to an
ordinary handler with `forge::openapi::describe`.

## Observability

The framework traces every request (a span named after the route template),
records request, job, connection pool, process and runtime metrics, and puts
`trace_id`/`span_id` on every log line of a request. Telemetry is on and
exports nothing until you choose a path; the variables and metric names are
Forge-Go's ([observability guide](https://github.com/rocketinsidecorp/forge-rust/blob/main/docs/observability.md)):

```bash
# Prometheus text on a separate, private listener (never on the app port)
FORGE_TELEMETRY_METRICS_ADDR=127.0.0.1:9464 cargo run
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:9464/metrics | grep http_server_request_duration_seconds_count
# Spans in the development log
FORGE_TELEMETRY_EXPORTER=log cargo run
# Traces, metrics and logs to an OpenTelemetry Collector (OTLP/HTTP)
FORGE_TELEMETRY_EXPORTER=otlp FORGE_TELEMETRY_LOGS_EXPORTER=otlp \
  FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4318 cargo run
# Off entirely
FORGE_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=false cargo run
```

Production requires an `https` collector endpoint; API keys go in
`FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_HEADERS` (`authorization=Bearer%20TOKEN`), which is
redacted everywhere. Register application metrics and spans through
`composition.telemetry()` in `src/bootstrap`.
%%if docker%%
In a container, bind the metrics listener to `0.0.0.0:9464`, confirm it with
`FORGE_TELEMETRY_METRICS_PUBLIC=true`, and do not publish that port.

### Observability stack: Grafana, Prometheus, Tempo and Loki

`compose.observability.yaml` adds a preconfigured stack to the compose
project: an OpenTelemetry Collector, Prometheus (metrics), Tempo (traces),
Loki (logs) and Grafana with the data sources and a starter dashboard already
provisioned. It is opt-in: `docker compose up` does not start it. Everything
stays on your machine and every published port is bound to `127.0.0.1`.

```bash
# 1. Start it (Grafana refuses to start without an admin password you choose)
export GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD="$(openssl rand -base64 18)"   # or put it in .env
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.observability.yaml up --build

# 2. Generate some data
curl http://127.0.0.1:3000/

# 3. Open Grafana: http://127.0.0.1:3001, user admin, the password above
#    Dashboards > "Forge application"  request rate, latency, errors, connection pool, process and Tokio runtime, jobs, outbox, logs, traces
#    Explore > Tempo                   traces; "Logs for this span" opens the matching log lines
#    Explore > Loki                    logs; a trace_id links to its trace
#    Explore > Prometheus              metrics, also at http://127.0.0.1:9090

# 4. Stop it (keeps the data); add -v to delete traces, logs and metrics too
# compose interpolates the whole file, so GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD must be set for down too
docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.observability.yaml down
```

The app sends traces, metrics and logs to the collector over OTLP (the
overlay sets `FORGE_TELEMETRY_EXPORTER=otlp`, `FORGE_TELEMETRY_LOGS_EXPORTER=otlp`
and, because the image runs in `production` and the collector is on the
compose network, `FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT=true`), which forwards
them to Tempo, Prometheus and Loki. Grafana is on host port 3001 because the
app uses 3000; set `GRAFANA_PORT` or `PROMETHEUS_PORT` to use other ports. The
app logs what it or the framework logs (startup, errors, your `tracing` calls);
each line logged inside a request carries `trace_id` and `span_id`, which is
what ties logs and traces together. Output that does not go through `tracing`
(a crash's stack trace, `println!`) is only in `docker compose logs`. The stack
keeps seven days of data and is meant for development, not production. To
export from an app started with `cargo run` while the stack runs:

```bash
FORGE_TELEMETRY_SERVICE_NAME=%%APP_NAME%% FORGE_TELEMETRY_EXPORTER=otlp \
  FORGE_TELEMETRY_LOGS_EXPORTER=otlp FORGE_TELEMETRY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4318 cargo run
```
%%end%%

## Credentials

Secrets that do not belong in `.env` files live encrypted in
`config/credentials/<environment>.json.enc`, which is committed. The key
stays out of git: `config/credentials/development.key` (created by `forge new`,
mode 0600) or the `FORGE_MASTER_KEY` variable in deployed environments.

```bash
forge credentials edit                          # edit development credentials in $EDITOR
forge credentials show                          # structure only; values are redacted
forge credentials init --environment production # new key and encrypted file for production
FORGE_MASTER_KEY="$(cat config/credentials/production.key)"  # what the deployed app needs
```

Back up every key; without it the encrypted file cannot be read. Read values
in `src/bootstrap` with `forge::credentials::open` (outside development and
test a missing file, key or required value stops startup). Encrypt a column
with `forge::encryption`; its key ring is already in the credentials:

```rust
let credentials = forge::credentials::open(
    "production",
    forge::credentials::Options::default().with_required(["encryption"]),
)?;
let cipher = forge::encryption::Cipher::new(&credentials.key_ring(&["encryption"])?)?;
let ssn = cipher.attribute("people", "ssn"); // a TEXT column, bound to its row id
let stored = ssn.seal(&id.to_string(), &"123-45-6789".to_owned())?;
let plain: String = ssn.open(&id.to_string(), &stored)?;
```

%%if docker%%
## Container

```bash
docker build -t %%APP_NAME%% .
%%if database%%
%%if !sqlite%%
docker compose up --build     # %%DATABASE_LABEL%%, one-shot migrations, then app
%%end%%
%%if sqlite%%
docker compose up --build     # one-shot migrations on the sqlite-data volume, then app
%%end%%
%%end%%
%%if !database%%
docker run --rm -p 3000:3000 %%APP_NAME%%
docker compose up --build
%%end%%
```

The image is built with `cargo build --release --locked`, so commit
`Cargo.lock`. The runtime stage is distroless and runs as uid 65532; the Docker
health check runs `%%APP_NAME%% healthcheck`.
%%if database%%

%%if postgresql%%
The Compose PostgreSQL credentials are development-only. It creates a dedicated
`app_migrator` role that owns schema changes and a separate `app_runtime` role
without schema ownership or `BYPASSRLS`. The database is stored in the named
`db-data` volume, and the app starts only after the database is healthy and
migrations complete successfully.

Tenant-sensitive persistence should open transactions through
`infrastructure::database::begin_tenant_transaction`. It installs the
authorized tenant/principal as transaction-local PostgreSQL settings; RLS policies
can read them with `current_setting('forge.tenant_id', true)` and
`current_setting('forge.principal_id', true)`.

`infrastructure::security` provides the concrete authentication mechanisms:
Argon2id password hashing (m=19456 KiB, t=2, p=1) on Tokio blocking workers,
independent 256-bit session/CSRF tokens from the OS CSPRNG, SHA-256 persistence
digests, a Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite=Lax `__Host-forge_session` cookie, generic
login denials, automatic Argon2id policy upgrades after successful verification,
request session lookup and CSRF enforcement for unsafe methods. Password rehashes
use optimistic credential versions so a concurrent password change or disable
cannot be overwritten. The bearer cookie and CSRF token are separate secrets;
never place the CSRF token
inside the HttpOnly session cookie.
%%end%%
%%if mysql_family%%

The Compose %%DATABASE_LABEL%% credentials are development-only (random root
password, application account `app`). The data lives in the named `db-data` volume,
and the app starts only after the database is healthy and migrations complete
successfully.
%%end%%
%%if sqlite%%

The SQLite file lives in the named `sqlite-data` volume mounted at `/data`, owned by
uid 65532 (the image's runtime user). The one-shot `migrate` service and the app share
it; run a single app replica per volume.
%%end%%
%%end%%
%%end%%
